# Prisma migrations are CHECKSUMMED, so their bytes are part of their identity.
#
# Prisma records a SHA-256 of each migration.sql in `_prisma_migrations` when it
# applies one, and compares it on every later `migrate dev`. A mismatch is read
# as "somebody edited an applied migration" and the answer is to demand a full
# database reset.
#
# On a machine with core.autocrlf=true — the Windows default, and what this repo
# is developed on — git checks these files out with CRLF while the recorded
# checksums were taken over LF. Nothing about the SQL changed; the file simply
# came back off disk in a different encoding of the same lines, and every
# migration in the repo read as edited. That is the whole of the long-standing
# "prisma wants to reset the DB" problem here.
#
# Pinning them to LF makes the working copy match what was hashed, and keeps it
# matching on the next fresh clone.
backend/prisma/migrations/**/*.sql text eol=lf
